{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1291510-am-i-misinterpreting-visage-great-and-terrible","id":1291510,"title":"Am I misinterpreting 'Visage great and terrible'?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-02-24T06:01:26-08:00","id":1291510,"title":"Am I misinterpreting 'Visage great and terrible'?","content":"I feel like I'm misunderstanding something about this boon (Hero p.243). There is no Clash of Wills or anything else that would let an enemy resist its effects. If a player uses it at the start of combat, then as a GM I have two options: I can either say 'Okay, never mind, they turn tail and run like the wind.' or I can deal with 2c on any action by the antagonists for the entire scene. Most other boons that introduce complications limit it to certain actions like:<br /> Noticing the Scion (Walk Unnoticed)<br /> Influencing the target (Stony Heart)<br /> Stealing your ride (Chariot of the Gods)<br /> etc... <br /> <br /> Many effects don't specify the consequences for failing to buy off a complication but that's okay if it happens once. Visage great and terrible will likely trigger over a dozen times in even a slightly larger combat. How would you handle coming up with so many complications that aren't just 'They hit you but wet their pants in the process'? <br /> <br /> This is one of those boons that makes me think complications are just subtracted from successes. Stony Heart is another - With how complications currently work, it doesn't make it any harder to influence the target, only introducing downsides to attempts that can never actually prevent success.<br /> Is this maybe a relic from a former version where complication just meant negative enhancement? <br /> <br /> Any input would be appreciated.<br /> Thanks","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13477-vezalchemyst","name":"VeZAlchemyst","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-02-27T12:19:35-08:00","id":1292567,"title":"","content":"&quot;+2 Complication on any action that does not somehow help them flee your presence.&quot;<br /> <br /> There are lots of ways someone get around such a complication, especially if they have a decently ranged weapon. Ranged attack followed by movement away fluffed as a tactical retreat with covering fire would meet that requirement. Heck, if the ugly scion has the victim somehow cornered, then even melee attacks could easily be exempt from the complication. Honestly, the ease when I first read through that particular power, my first thought was &quot;nice implementation in theory, but the ease with which one can avoid the complication with pure roleplay makes it mechanically sub-par and best suited to fluffy mid-opp stories.&quot;<br /> <br /> If anything, Enthralling performance and Overwhelming Chaos are much closer to the problem that you are describing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1576-mjorkk","name":"mjorkk","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}